research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-48-{"id":"lem-topological-group-translations-and-inversion","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The BIU notes state that left and right shifts and inversion are homeomorphisms. The proof expands continuity and inverse formulas.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-49-{"id":"def-left-and-right-uniformities-of-a-topological-group","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. BIU gives exactly these entourage formulas under an explicit Hausdorff hypothesis, while the item correctly defines them for arbitrary, possibly non-Hausdorff topological groups. Dropping Hausdorff is standard established mathematics, but it is a genuine hypothesis delta and no matching source surfaced. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The displayed left and right entourage formulas are the standard uniformities of a topological group. Their construction uses only the group operations and identity neighbourhoods; Hausdorffness controls separation, not existence, so the broader domain is established.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-50-{"id":"thm-topological-group-uniformities-induce-the-given-topology","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. BIU states the natural group uniformities induce the group topology in its Hausdorff uniformity section. The same proof works for arbitrary topological groups and inversion interchanges the two, but no located source states this exact non-Hausdorff package. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Left and right uniformities inducing the original group topology, with inversion interchanging them, is a standard topological-group theorem. The neighbourhood-translation proof is unchanged without Hausdorffness, so the local package is an established generalization.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-51-{"id":"def-upper-and-roelcke-uniformities-of-a-topological-group","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf","https://homepages.math.uic.edu/~rosendal/PapersWebsite/Coarse-Geometry-Book23.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. BIU and Rosendal define the upper join and Roelcke meet with the displayed basic entourages for Hausdorff or Polish groups. The formulas extend unchanged to arbitrary topological groups, a standard convention, but the published domain is broader than the located sources. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The upper join and Roelcke meet, with intersection and left-right composite bases, are standard topological-group uniformities. The formulas require no separation axiom, so extending their usual sourced domain to arbitrary topological groups is established mathematics.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-52-{"id":"cor-topological-groups-are-completely-regular","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Uniformizability of every topological group and complete regularity of uniformizable spaces are standard, but the item states their precise DC-dependent composition under the non-Hausdorff convention. No source states that exact package. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Every topological group carries its canonical uniformities, and uniformizable spaces are completely regular in the library's separation-free convention. Their composition, with DC explicitly exposed for the latter implication, is a standard corollary rather than a novel theorem.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
