items/lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages.md-45-
items/lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages.md-46-1.1 Given a symmetric entourage $E$, choose a symmetric $R$ with $R^{\circ2}\subseteq E$, and then a symmetric $D$ with $D^{\circ2}\subseteq R$. Since every entourage contains the diagonal, $D\subseteq R$, and hence $$ D^{\circ3}\subseteq R^{\circ2}\subseteq E. $$ Thus there exists a symmetric $D\subseteq E$ with $D^{\circ3}\subseteq E$. [L1, construct]
items/lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages.md-47-
items/lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages.md-48-2.1 The relation $E\mathrel R D$ meaning that $D$ is symmetric, $D\subseteq E$, and $D\circ D\circ D\subseteq E$ is serial by step 1.1. [step 1.1]
items/lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages.md-49-
items/lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages.md:50:3.1 Choose a symmetric entourage $E_1\subseteq U$ using [L1]. Dependent choice applied to the serial relation of step 2.1 starting at $E_1$ gives $E_1,E_2,\ldots$. Adjoin $E_0=X\times X$; then $E_1^{\circ3}\subseteq X\times X=E_0$, and all the required properties hold. [step 2.1, L1, L2, discharge-construct] ∎
--
