items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-46-
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-47-**Proof technique:** constructive.
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-48-
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md:49:1.1 Choose an entourage $U$ with $U[x]\cap C=\varnothing$ by [L5]. Using dependent choice, take a normal sequence with $E_0=X\times X$ and $E_1\subseteq U$. [L4, L5, choose, construct]
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-50-
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-51-2.1 Let $p$ be the controlled pseudometric from [L1]. Since $\{p\le1/4\}\subseteq E_1\subseteq U$, every $y\in C$ satisfies $p(x,y)>1/4$. [step 1.1, L1]
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-52-
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-53-3.1 Put $g(y)=\min\{1,4p(x,y)\}$. The reverse triangle inequality for a pseudometric gives $$ |p(x,y)-p(x,z)|\le p(y,z), $$ and truncation at $1$ does not increase absolute differences. Hence, for every $\varepsilon>0$, the entourage $\{(y,z):p(y,z)<\varepsilon/4\}$ forces $|g(y)-g(z)|<\varepsilon$; $g$ is uniformly continuous. Also $g(x)=0$ and $g[C]=\{1\}$ by step 2.1, so $1-g$ has the orientation required in [L2]. [step 2.1, L1, construct]
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-54-
items/lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-completely-regular.md-55-4.1 By [L3], $1-g$ is continuous, so [L2] proves complete regularity. [step 3.1, L2, L3, discharge-construct] ∎
--
